@article{yokotaValueInformationAnalysis2004,
  title = {Value of Information Analysis in Environmental Health Risk Management Decisions: Past, Present, and Future},
  shorttitle = {Value of Information Analysis in Environmental Health Risk Management Decisions},
  author = {Yokota, Fumie and Thompson, Kimberly M.},
  year = 2004,
  journal = {Risk Analysis: an International Journal},
  volume = {24},
  number = {3},
  pages = {NaN-NaN},
  publisher = {Wiley Online Library},
  doi = {10.1111/j.0272-4332.2004.00464.x},
  langid = {english},
  keywords = {read CW},
  note = {\section{Annotations\\
(6/16/2025, 8:31:38 PM)}

\par
``Experts agree that value of information (VOI) analyses provide useful insights in risk management decisions. However, applications in environmental health risk management (EHRM) remain largely demonstrative thus far because of the complexity in modeling and solving VOI problems.'' (Yokota and Thompson, 2004, p. 635)
\par
Export Date: 05 May 2024; Cited By: 145
\par
Risk Analysis, Vol. 24, No. 3, 2004
\par
Value of Information Analysis in Environmental Health Risk
\par
Management Decisions: Past, Present, and Future
\par
Fumie Yokota1 and Kimberly M. Thompson2{$\ast$}
\par
Experts agree that value of information (VOI) analyses provide useful insights in risk manage-
\par
ment decisions. However, applications in environmental health risk management (EHRM)
\par
remain largely demonstrative thus far because of the complexity in modeling and solving
\par
VOI problems. Based on this comprehensive review of all VOI applications published in the
\par
peer-reviewed literature of such applications, the complexity of solving VOI problems with
\par
continuous probability distributions as inputs in models emerges as the main barrier to greater
\par
use of VOI although simulation allows analysts to solve more complex and realistic problems.
\par
Several analytical challenges that inhibit greater use of VOI techniques include issues related
\par
to modeling decisions, valuing outcomes, and characterizing uncertain and variable model
\par
inputs appropriately. This comprehensive review of methods for modeling and solving VOI
\par
problems for applications related to EHRM provides the first synthesis of important method-
\par
ological advances in the field. The insights provide risk analysts and decision scientists with
\par
some guidance on how to structure and solve VOI problems focused on evaluating opportuni-
\par
ties to collect better information to improve EHRM decisions. They further suggest the need
\par
for some efforts to standardize approaches and develop some prescriptive guidance for VOI
\par
analysts similar to existing guidelines for conducting cost-effectiveness analyses.
\par
KEY WORDS: Bayesian decision theory; environmental health; risk analysis; risk management; value of
\par
information
\par
1. INTRODUCTION
\par
Value of information (VOI) analysis evaluates the
\par
benefit of collecting additional information to reduce
\par
or eliminate uncertainty in a specific decision making
\par
context. As noted in one of the earliest published VOI
\par
applications, ``no theory that involves just the proba-
\par
bilities of outcomes without considering their conse-
\par
1 Office of Information and Regulatory Affairs, Office of Manage-
\par
ment and Budget, Executive Office of the President, Washington,
\par
DC., USA.
\par
2 Department of Health Policy and Management, Harvard School
\par
of Public Health, 677 Huntington Ave., 3rd Floor, Boston, MA
\par
02115, USA.
\par
{$\ast$} Address correspondence to Kimberly Thompson, Department of
\par
Health Policy and Management, Harvard School of Public Health,
\par
677 Huntington Ave., 3rd Floor, Boston, MA 02115, USA; tel:
\par
617-432-4285; kimt@hsph.harvard.edu.
\par
635 quences could possibly be adequate in describing the
\par
importance of uncertainty to a decision maker.''(1:26)
\par
VOI analysis makes explicit any expected potential
\par
losses from errors in decision making due to uncer-
\par
tainty and identifies the ``best'' information collection
\par
strategy as one that leads to the greatest net benefit
\par
to the decision maker (DM). The recent Presidential/
\par
Congressional Commission on Risk Assessment and
\par
Risk Management (1997) noted that ``when stakes in
\par
a decision are large and the uncertainties complex,
\par
risk managers or their technical staffs may find it use-
\par
ful to experiment with formal value-of-information
\par
tools.''(2:92) Although some of the earliest publications
\par
on decision analysis introduced methods for model-
\par
ing and solving VOI analyses decades ago,(1,3--7) un-
\par
like other economic analytic methods such as cost-
\par
benefit analysis and cost-effectiveness analysis, very
\par
0272-4332/04/0100-0635\$22.00/1 C ⃝ 2004 Society for Risk Analysis636 Yokota and Thompson
\par
few VOI applications in environmental health risk
\par
management (EHRM) exist. Moreover, recent analy-
\par
sis of VOI applications shows the tendency of articles
\par
to focus on demonstrating the usefulness of the VOI
\par
approach rather than on applications to actual man-
\par
agement decisions.(8)
\par
The lack of VOI applications in actual EHRM de-
\par
cisions appears to arise in part from the inherent com-
\par
plexities in modeling the underlying probabilistic risk
\par
assessment and decision analysis. The analyst must
\par
model all relevant sets of actions and information col-
\par
lection strategies available to the DM, capture all sig-
\par
nificant consequences of each action given all possible
\par
states of the world, value those outcomes in a com-
\par
mon metric, and characterize important uncertainty,
\par
variability, and the accuracy of information to be col-
\par
lected by fitting probability distributions to available
\par
information. In addition, for VOI calculations ana-
\par
lysts cannot model the risk assessment problem using
\par
point estimates. Instead, they must characterize the
\par
uncertainty quantitatively, and they cannot rely on a
\par
general monotonic relationship between VOI and ac-
\par
tion flexibility (i.e., increasing actions available to the
\par
DM) or the level of initial uncertainty in the prior dis-
\par
tribution since no such relationship exists.(9) With the
\par
increasing evolution of simulation strategies, exam-
\par
ples of fairly complex VOI problems now appear in
\par
the literature. However, many analytical challenges
\par
remain and Ron Howard's prediction in 1967 still
\par
rings true:``[I]t is inevitable that in the future both
\par
technical and managerial decision-makers will em-
\par
ploy formal logical methods in decision-making. The
\par
transition will probably be painful.''(6:60) Remarkably,
\par
no articles currently provide a comprehensive review
\par
of the existing VOI applications for EHRM issues,
\par
and this analysis fills the void by providing a critical
\par
review of 16 VOI applications.(10--25)
\par
2. MODELING VALUE OF
\par
INFORMATION ANALYSES
\par
The expected value of perfect information
\par
(EVPI) represents the value of completely eliminat-
\par
ing uncertainty (i.e., collecting information with per-
\par
fect accuracy). For a risk-neutral expected utility max-
\par
imizer with a linear or exponential utility function,(26)
\par
Equation (1) shows the EVPI about an uncertain
\par
input s:
\par
EVPI=
\par
