EVSI represents the difference between the expected
\par
utility of taking the optimal action based on the pos-
\par
terior probability of s given experimental informa-
\par
tion t, and the expected utility from taking the opti-
\par
mal decision given only the prior information about s.
\par
In general, the predictive density of the information
\par
sampled (Equation (4)) lacks a closed-form solution,
\par
except for uncertainty expressed as a set of discrete
\par
probabilities or for select model structures featuring
\par
likelihood functions with conjugate priors.(29) In com-
\par
parison, EVPI represents a simpler calculation than
\par
the EVSI and serves as a useful theoretical upper
\par
bound for the value of additional information for a
\par
particular decision context. All EHRM VOI analyses
\par
require several key elements including:
\par
1. A set of available actions and information col-
\par
lection strategies. This may include discrete ac-
\par
tions or continuous decision variables.
\par
2. A risk model with variability and uncertainty
\par
in its inputs clearly defined within the context
\par
of the decision.(27,30) This represents a par-
\par
ticularly important aspect of EHRM mod-
\par
els since, unlike uncertainty, true variability
\par
cannot be reduced by obtaining more infor-
\par
mation. As the National Research Council's
\par
Committee on Risk Assessment of Hazardous
\par
Air Pollutants stated:``uncertainty forces de-
\par
cision makers to judge how probable it is that
\par
risks will be overestimated or underestimated
\par
for every member of the exposed population,
\par
whereas variability forces them to cope with
\par
the certainty that different individuals will be
\par
subjected to risks both above and below any
\par
reference point one chooses.''(31:237) For dis-
\par
crete inputs, analysts may use a discrete set of
\par
value-probability pairs, while for continuous
\par
inputs they may use distribution functions
\par
uniquely defined by parameters or empirical
\par
distribution functions based on a data set of
\par
observed values or based on subjective judg-
\par
ments from experts (which they characterize
\par
by employing a variety of techniques).(32--34)
\par
Analysts must also characterize any depen-
\par
dence between multiple inputs in their joint
\par
distribution, a critical consideration in VOI
\par
analyses since dependencies significantly com-
\par
plicate VOI calculations.(35)
\par
3. Values for the risk outcomes. For societal de-
\par
cisions and those that impact large organiza-
\par
tions that manage a portfolio of risks, typi-
\par
cally analysts assume risk neutrality and rely
\par
on linear utility with valuation monetized.
\par
This necessitates often controversial choices
\par
about discounting and monetary equivalent
\par
values of health outcomes (e.g., the value of
\par
a statistical life).
\par
All estimates for EVSI also require estimates of
\par
the accuracy of future information prior to its col-
\par
lection. As discussed by Brand and Small(36) within
\par
the context of the continuum from release of a sub-
\par
stance into the environment to any ultimate health
\par
outcomes, numerous opportunities may exist to ob-
\par
tain information along the continuum and the actual
\par
point where the analyst obtains information may dif-
\par
ferentially impact overall uncertainty about the final
\par
result.
\par
3. SOLVING VALUE OF
\par
INFORMATION ANALYSES
\par
3.1. Overview
\par
Analysts solve the simplest VOI applications
\par
(i.e., with uncertainty characterized for independent
\par
model inputs using discrete distributions) with``pencil
\par
and paper'' by rolling back the decision tree. However,
\par
for more complex models they tend to use off-the-
\par
shelf software using decision trees or influence dia-
\par
grams, or they write their own code to solve large
\par
and complex problems given the exponential com-
\par
putational effort associated with using a discrete ap-
\par
proximation strategy. For a small number of carefully
\par
chosen models with continuous distributions, sim-
\par
ple applications exist with closed-form solutions that
\par
yield exact values. In general, however, problems with
\par
continuous inputs prove more complex and they lead
\par
the analyst to use one of several numerical approxi-
\par
mation methods.
\par
