4.1. Modeling the Set of Available Actions
\par
and Information-Collection Options
\par
The discrete analyses(10--14) focused on EVSI from
\par
toxicological testing in determining the carcinogenic-
\par
ity of chemicals. Since these articles illustrated the
\par
VOI framework for a generic management decision
\par
rather than informing a specific real decision, they
\par
considered the very limited set of only two regula-
\par
tory actions (i.e., do nothing or do something). These
\par
analyses assumed that regulators would follow a hy-
\par
pothetical, preset, certain control decision if testing
\par
the chemical suggested carcinogenicity. Most of these
\par
analyses consider only two information-collection
\par
strategies. Two articles by Lave and Omenn(10,11) con-
\par
sidered the value of regulating chemicals based on the
\par
results of a short-term test for carcinogenicity com-
\par
pared to allowing chemicals to go unregulated. Lave
\par
et al.
\par
(12) evaluated the benefit of conducting rodent
\par
bioassays to no testing. In contrast, Olson(13) com-
\par
pared acting on prior information to conducting a mu-
\par
tagenicity test and then evaluating whether to collect
\par
bioassay information or not. Omenn et al.
\par
(14) went a
\par
step further and compared 13 different approaches
\par
developed by various researchers that combine infor-
\par
mation on structure-activity relationships, short-term
\par
tests, sub-chronic rodent assays, and/or expert judg-
\par
ment for predicting the results of a lifetime rodent
\par
bioassay.
\par
The analyses that discretized continuous uncer-
\par
tain inputs represent much more complex decision
\par
problems. The earliest two analyses focused on char-
\par
acterizing model uncertainty by measuring the value
\par
of perfect information, and they did not evaluate
\par
specific information-collection schemes. North and
\par
Merkhofer(15) calculated the EVPI from resolving un-
\par
certainties in choosing four alternative strategies to
\par
control pollution emissions for three representative
\par
power plant types. Finkel and Evans(16) described
\par
a VOI framework for environmental management
\par
and calculated the EVPI and EVPXI for dose of a
\par
hypothetical risk management problem with three
\par
alternatives.
\par
The next three analyses, on the other hand, evalu-
\par
ated specific information-gathering strategies. Evans
\par
et al.
\par
(17) modeled homeowner's EVSXI from mon-
\par
itoring radon in the home in choosing one of five
\par
remediation strategies (with two of the five always
\par
dominated and not included in the final calculation).
\par
Reichard and Evans(18) considered the EVPI, EVPXI,
\par
and EVSXI of four options for monitoring ground-
\par
water for arsenic in a decision to install or not install
\par
point of use drinking water filtration system. Taylor
\par
et al.
\par
(19) assessed the EVPI and EVSI of the animal
\par
bioassay's ability to determine magnitude of cancer-
\par
causing potential above and beyond determining its
\par
carcinogenicity. Using the simplistic choice of actions
\par
(i.e., act or not), they provided general insights about
\par
the three strategies for collecting toxicological infor-
\par
mation: collect none, use only a subchronic bioassay,
\par
or conduct additional long-term bioassay.
\par
The last analysis in this group evaluated the bene-
\par
fit of a two-stage approach to pollution control, which
\par
allowed the incorporation of information collected
\par
from the first stage in the decision at the second
\par
stage.(20) Chao et al.
\par
(20) calculated EVSI from wait-
\par
ing for more information to choose the optimal levels
\par
of control of both nitrogen oxides and volatile organic
\par
compounds to reduce tropospheric ozone based on di-
\par
viding the level of emission reduction into five levels
\par
for each pollutant (0, 20, 40, 60, and 80\%) for each
\par
stage, which yielded 625 action scenarios.
\par
The analyses that used simulation include two
\par
that considered only two action alternatives, and two
\par
others that did not consider specific information-
\par
collections strategies. Dakins et al.
\par
(21,22) evaluated the
\par
value of resolving uncertainty about PCB contamina-
\par
tion in fish to assist in choosing the optimal level of
\par
remediation of contaminated sediments in New Bed-
\par
ford Harbor, MA. Dakins et al.
\par
(21) evaluated EVPI for
\par
the model and Dakins et al.
\par
(22) conducted a prepos-
\par
terior analysis to evaluate the EVSI from sampling
\par
2, 5, and 10 randomly selected flounder from New
\par
Bedford Harbor. Thompson and Evans(23) calculated
\par
the EVPI and EVPXI from collecting national ex-
\par
posure information about perchloroethylene (perc)
\par
used in dry cleaning. Unlike other applications, this
\par
analysis compared regulating perc exposure at three
\par
different levels of decision making: individual dry-
\par
cleaning facilities, by particular dry-cleaning machine
\par
category (defined by type and size), and by particular
\par
machine type, with several control options for each
\par
type of machine (a total of 11).
\par
The remaining two analyses, in contrast, focused
\par
on optimal information collection, and simple choice
\par
of action or no action. Lin et al.
\par
(24) evaluated the
\par
EVSI from measuring radon concentrations in private
