4.2. Characterizing Variability and Uncertainty
\par
The discrete analyses represented simple mod-
\par
els that did not include any characterization of vari-
\par
ability. They focused on characterizing the value of
\par
resolving uncertainty about a single dichotomous in-
\par
put (i.e., carcinogenicity of a chemical or not) and
\par
used hypothetical point estimates of prior probability
\par
of carcinogenicity and point estimates for the like-
\par
lihood of test results based on empirical data. The
\par
articles by Lave and Omenn(10,11) assumed a range
\par
of point estimates for the sensitivity and specificity
\par
of rodent bioassays in predicting human carcinogens.
\par
Lave et al.
\par
(12) assumed point estimates for both sensi-
\par
tivity and specificity based on empirical evidence for
\par
each type of testing. Olson(13) also used a hypothet-
\par
ical prior but disaggregated likelihood into the four
\par
categories of possible bioassay results established by
\par
the U.S. National Toxicology Program (NTP), and as-
\par
sumed discrete probability values based on available
\par
empirical evidence. Omenn et al.
\par
(14) compared 13 ap-
\par
proaches for predicting results of a lifetime rodent
\par
bioassay (carcinogenic or not) developed through the
\par
Carcinogenic Prediction Challenge sponsored by the
\par
NTP. They used a hypothetical point estimate of prior
\par
probability and the sensitivity and specificity values
\par
implied by the strategies for 44 chemicals.
\par
The analyses that used a discretization approach
\par
tended to include only a couple of continuous un-
\par
certain inputs, with only a couple of them explicitly
\par
addressing variability.(15,17) These studies used log-
\par
normal distributions for prior distributions and/or
\par
likelihood functions that allowed analytical solutions
\par
for the product of inputs and posterior distributions.
\par
The EVPI analysis by North and Merkhofer(15) eval-
\par
uated the value of simultaneously resolving two un-
\par
certainties in the model: how a unit of emission trans-
\par
lates to ambient concentration and the total health
\par
cost per unit increase in suspended sulfate concen-
\par
tration, which they assumed as independent in the
\par
analysis. The authors relied on their subjective judg-
\par
ment of extreme values to represent the 5th and 95th
\par
percentile points on the cumulative probability dis-
\par
tribution. They provided a sketch of the cumulative
\par
distribution for the ambient sulfate concentration in-
\par
crement and assumed a log-normal distribution for
\par
the total health cost. To account for variability in
\par
the total health cost on local population density and
\par
fuel-burning technology, they solved the optimization
\par
problem for three types of power plants: an existing
\par
coal plant in a rural area, a new construction in a rural
\par
area, and an oil-burning plant (originally designed for
\par
coal) in an urban East Coast location.
\par
Finkel and Evans(16) modeled uncertainty about
\par
the health risk from a contaminant as the product of
\par
two components: dose and exposure. They assumed
\par
a log-normal distribution for both components and
\par
consequently the uncertainty about risk was also log-
\par
normal. They used hypothetical parameter values to
\par
illustrate how EVPI varied with different prior beliefs
\par
about the uncertainty in risk. Evans et al.
\par
(17) used the
\par
approach established by Finkel and Evans(16) to char-
\par
acterize the uncertainty in exposure to radon in indi-
\par
vidual homes and its potency in causing cancer. They
\par
developed log-normal distributions for both compo-
\par
nents using formal expert judgment elicitation. One
\par
expert set parameter values for the prior distribu-
\par
tion for exposure based on previously available mon-
\par
itoring information (e.g., regional monitoring data,
\par
monitoring in a neighbor's home), and accounted for
\par
variability in radon exposure by setting different pa-
\par
rameter values based on region of the country and
\par
characteristic of the home. A different expert pro-
\par
vided parameters for a log-normal distribution of ex-
\par
cess relative risk of cancer to the general population
\par
based on epidemiological data available for occupa-
\par
tional exposure of radon to miners. Evans et al.
\par
(17) in-
\par
cluded additional variability in potential benefits from
\par
monitoring by analyzing the VOI to household of four
\par
representative demographic compositions.
\par
Reichard and Evans(18) used a similar approach
\par
and included two uncertain inputs characterized by
\par
a log-normal distribution: the potency of arsenic in
\par
causing cancer and the exposure to arsenic in well
\par
water. They assumed a log-normal distribution for po-
\par
tency, and fit a multistage dose-response model to epi-
\par
demiological data to estimate the geometric standard
\par
deviation of potency. For exposure to arsenic, how-
\par
ever, they used simulation to propagate uncertainty
\par
in a hydrogeologic model with five uncertain inputs
