4.3. Modeling and Valuing Outcomes
\par
Table III summarizes the health outcomes eval-
\par
uated by the analyses and values used to calculate
\par
VOI. As the table shows, the discrete analyses used
\par
a simple cost-benefit framework, and did not use so-
\par
phisticated measures of outcomes. Most of the dis-
\par
crete analyses(10--12,14) evaluated a lump sum for two
\par
consequences: a regulatory false positive assumed to
\par
impose a net cost to society from unnecessarily regu-
\par
lating a noncarcinogen of \$1 million, or a regulatory
\par
false negative that imposes a net cost to society from
\par
not regulating a carcinogen of \$10 million. These an-
\par
alysts used the same baseline values consistently in
\par
their articles published between 1986 and 1995. None
\par
of these analyses reported a discount rate or whether
\par
the analysts adjusted the monetary values to a com-
\par
mon year dollar. In contrast, Olson(13) calculated the
\par
number of cases of cancer from a hypothetical unreg-
\par
ulated carcinogen and valued them at \$2 million (1986
\par
dollars) using a discount rate of zero.
\par
The articles that discretized continuous uncertain
\par
inputs tended to develop much more sophisticated
\par
cost-benefit analyses and to use more refined mea-
\par
sures of outcomes. North and Merkhofer(15) identified
\par
and valued a variety of health endpoints from air pol-
\par
