\par
Four analyses(16--19) modeled dose response and
\par
exposure to estimate the risk of developing cancer
\par
from various carcinogens and used a nominal value
\par
of life ranging from \$1 million to \$10 million (with
\par
an even wider range in sensitivity analyses). Since the
\par
earliest analysis used the smallest baseline value and
\par
the latest used the largest, the difference in real value
\par
appears greater, although we cannot calculate it ex-
\par
actly because these analyses did not report what year
\par
dollar the values represented. The discount rate in
\par
these analyses ranged from 3 to 5\%. Chao et al.
\par
(20)
\par
did not specifically measure a health endpoint, but in-
\par
stead estimated a lump-sum value of \$10 billion per
\par
year per ppm of peak ozone concentration exceeding
\par
0.12 ppm (in 1989 dollars).
\par
The analyses that used simulation as a solution
\par
strategy took much more varied approaches to mod-
\par
eling outcomes. Two analyses(23,25) used a risk assess-
\par
ment approach to evaluate the net societal costs from
\par
health damages caused by exposure to perc and CBD,
\par
respectively. Thompson and Evans(23) valued each
\par
premature death from cancer at \$3 million in 1989
\par
dollars, with a range of \$1 million to \$10 million in sen-
\par
sitivity analyses, and used a consistent discount rate of
\par
5\% in the base case analysis to account for time prefer-
\par
ence. Bartell et al.
\par
(25) valued a case of CBD prevented
\par
using four different estimates ranging from a low of
\par
\$12,200, which considered only future medical costs
\par
averted discounted at 7\%, to a high of \$16,300,000,
\par
which represents an upper value of a statistical life
\par
ignoring disease latencies and discounting (no year
\par
dollar given).
\par
In contrast, Lin et al.
\par
(24) modeled risks of cancer
\par
from radon exposure, but rather than using a specific
\par
value per life to drive the analysis, they used an action
\par
level as the benchmark, assuming that above the set
\par
level of exposure remediation should occur. They pre-
\par
determined the action level based on household com-
\par
position, a household's risk preference, and WTP for
\par
risk reduction. The objective focused on minimizing
\par
total cost, which included residual risk after remedi-
\par
ation. In the base case analysis, the authors chose the
\par
EPA's action level using an annual living area aver-
\par
age concentration of 4 pCi/L. Assuming a household
\par
consists of the average number of male and female
\par
smokers and never-smokers in the United States, the
\par
action level implies a value of \$210,000 per life. They
\par
used a discount rate of 5\% to account for time pref-
\par
erence, but did not report whether they adjusted all
\par
monetary values to a common year dollar.
\par
In contrast, Dakins et al.
\par
(21,22) focused on choos-
\par
ing an optimal level of dredging to minimize reme-
\par
diation costs while meeting a health-based standard
\par
for PCB concentration in fish set by the FDA. The
\par
analysis, which evaluated the cost of underremedia-
\par
tion in 1985 dollars and use a 0\% discount rate, as-
\par
sumed future perfect knowledge of the correct level
\par
of remediation, and it assumed that meeting the stan-
\par
dard implied no residual health risk. If insufficient
\par
remediation occurred, then this required additional
\par
remediation to meet the standard and led to penal-
\par
ties (e.g., the fishery remained closed for longer time
\par
and additional costs of remobilizing research and
\par
remediation efforts). These studies did not account
\par
for health risks from suboptimal dredging or any
\par
health risks that would exist either during or after
\par
remediation.
\par
5. REMAINING ANALYTICAL CHALLENGES
\par
The VOI analyses in EHRM decisions to date
\par
represent primarily demonstrations of the usefulness
\par
of approaching a management problem using a VOI
\par
framework. While simulation appears to allow ana-
\par
lysts to solve more complicated and realistic problems,
\par
important barriers remain.
\par
With respect to modeling, none of the previ-
\par
ous analyses addressed nonlinearity in risk models.
\par
Though analysts generally model cancer risks as a
\par
linear function of dose, current procedures for non-
\par
cancer risk assessment require the modeling of a
\par
threshold below which no detrimental effects occur.
\par
As discussed previously, when nonlinear inputs exist
\par
in the output value function, analysts cannot calcu-
\par
late the EVPXI with simulation using a simple substi-
\par
tution of expected value of that input. Further, none
\par
of the EHRM analyses included correlated uncertain
\par
input distributions or assessed the impact of depen-
\par
dence on information collected. This suggests that
\par
dealing with probabilistic dependencies remains one
\par
of the greatest challenges, as foreseen by Howard.(35)
\par
Not surprisingly, the challenges associated with
\par
obtaining appropriate data for characterizing model
\par
input distributions remain paramount.(32) Collecting
\par
additional information may lead to surprises that
\par
reveal incorrect basic assumptions, such as observ-
\par
ing input values outside the bounds of prior belief
\par
in those cases where the model used overconfident
\par
priors.(42,43) Characterizations based on expert judg-
\par
ment may be overly narrow due to tendency of both
\par
lay people and experts to be overconfident in their
\par
knowledge(44) and using more that one expert leads
\par
15396924, 2004, 3, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.0272-4332.2004.00464.x by Bonn University/Library, Wiley Online Library on [14/11/2023]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License648 Yokota and Thompson
\par
to issues related to combining and weighting the
\par
information from experts and dealing with their lack
\par
of independence.(45)
\par
Further efforts to improve characterization and
\par
distinction of variability and uncertainty promise to
\par
continue to improve VOI applications. As the case of
\par
mandatory passenger-side airbags in motor vehicles
\par
shows, significant consequences derive from ignoring
\par
variability in a decision analysis.(46) In addition, since
\par
more information does not reduce true variability, a
\par
distribution that combines uncertainty and variability
\par
in VOI analyses may yield inappropriate results and
\par
analysts must use care to treat the uncertainty and
\par
variability appropriately and transparently in the con-
\par
text of the desired characterization of risk.(27,47) For
\par
example, for a decision where a risk manager seeks
\par
to control the total number of cases of a disease, the
\par
analyst may collapse the variability in the population
\par
into the uncertainty in the model and that distribu-
\par
tion should represent the uncertainty about the mean
\par
(i.e., the standard error), not the standard deviation
\par
of individuals in the population.(23,27,32)
\par
The review suggests that analysts may not suf-
\par
ficiently deal with the issue of potential errors in
\par
VOI analyses stemming from numerical approxima-
\par
tion methods. Only one analysis, Dakins et al.,
\par
(23) re-
\par
ported multiple simulation results to show the ro-
\par
bustness of the VOI estimate, although the results
\par
by Thompson and Evans(24) actually represented the
\par
mean of 10 simulations of 10,000 iterations each. As
\par
computational capabilities of personal computers in-
\par
crease, large simulations become increasingly faster
\par
and cheaper and consequently the time required to
\par
set up the model in many cases now represents the
\par
slowest part of the process.
\par
Nonetheless, analysts must still evaluate the ben-
\par
efit of performing increasingly complex VOI analyses
\par
in the context of the actual decision(31) and recognize
\par
that even without formal analysis, the VOI framework
\par
can provide helpful insights for determining the ap-
\par
propriate balance between taking action and waiting
\par
for more information. As Howard stated:``[O]ne of
\par
the arts of the decision analyst is the art of know-
\par
ing how much and what kind of decision analysis to
\par
do. The degree of analysis can range from making
\par
simple lists to constructing giant interactive computer
\par
models. To be effective decision analysis must be`ap-
\par
propriate': the extent of the analysis must be suitable
\par
to the means and ends of the decision-maker.''(35:22)
\par
A National Research Council report more recently
\par
asserted that``value-of-information analysis can be
\par
of considerable use in the analytic-deliberative pro-
\par
cess''(48:111) and suggested that``risk characterization
\par
should be a decision-driven activity, directed toward
\par
informing choices and solving problems.''(48:155)
\par
Valuation issues also present important chal-
\par
lenges for analysts. Though economists calculate a
\par
societal value for averting premature morbidity and
\par
mortality, no widely agreed value exists.(49,50) Not sur-
\par
prisingly, the values analysts choose to use vary widely,
\par
leading to challenges since the choice of value of life
\par
can dramatically impact the VOI results.(23) In addi-
\par
tion, empirical evidence from both revealed prefer-
\par
ence and stated preference studies show that factors
\par
such as age, income, baseline mortality risk, and la-
\par
tency of the risk influence the value of statistical life
\par
(VSL), but only income shows a predictable, mono-
\par
tonic relationship.(51) Similarly, no consensus exists on
\par
what discount rate to use to reflect societal time pref-
\par
erence, an important model input for decisions related
\par
to diseases that may have long latencies, and latency
\par
in cancer remains a very difficult factor to include in
\par
risk and decision analytic models.
\par
For the societal perspective, analysts need to eval-
\par
uate all relevant contexts where DMs might find util-
\par
ity in information. VOI can inaccurately estimate the
\par
true societal value of perfect information if positive
\par
or negative externalities arise from the information
\par
collection while not explicitly modeled in the deci-
\par
sion. For example, toxicological information about
\par
one substance may be used for the specific regula-
\par
tory decision about controlling that substance, but it
\par
may also be used in models to predict toxicity of sub-
\par
stances with similar structures. While some analysts
\par
discuss this issue, we see no progress on developing
\par
strategies to deal with it in VOI analyses. Another
\par
continuing challenge relates to valuing nonmonetary
\par
outcomes, but perhaps one of the largest hurdles in-
\par
volves creating forums and processes for risk ana-
\par
lysts and economists to collaborate more effectively
\par
in developing integrated analyses to support EHRM
\par
decisions.(52)
\par
6. CONCLUSION
\par
Rigorous VOI analyses provide opportunities to
\par
evaluate strategies to collect information to improve
\par
EHRM decisions. This review of the methodology and
\par
applications shows that advances in computing tools
\par
allow analysts to tackle problems with greater com-
\par
plexity, although the literature still lacks``real'' appli-
\par
cations, probably due to a number of barriers. These
\par
barriers include the lack of guidance from EPA and
\par
others on criteria for standardizing EHRM risk and
\par
