\par
decision analyses, the lack of consensus on values to
\par
use for health outcomes, the lack of default probabil-
\par
ity distributions for frequently used inputs, and inex-
\par
perience of risk managers and communicators with
\par
using probabilistic risk results.(27,30) In addition, it re-
\par
mains analytically challenging to model decisions that
\par
use all available information, deal with nonlinear in-
\par
puts, and include correlation in input distributions
\par
and dependence in information collected. We sug-
\par
gest that an effort to bring analysts together to dis-
\par
cuss strategies for standardizing methods might pro-
\par
vide some useful guidance, similar to the experience
\par
in the field of clinical decision making when the U.S.
\par
Public Health Service convened the Panel on Cost-
\par
Effectiveness in Health and Medicine in 1993.(53) We
\par
expect that future reviews may also play an impor-
\par
tant role in synthesizing the experiences of analysts,
\par
encouraging analysts to publish their work in peer-
\par
reviewed journals, and demonstrating the state of the
\par
field.
