\par
VOI ANALYSIS
\par
In an expected utility maximization framework, VOI
\par
represents the difference between the expected utility
\par
of the optimal action given new information and the
\par
expected utility of the optimal action given informa-
\par
tion available prior to collecting additional informa-
\par
tion3--8 assuming a linear or exponential utility function
\par
and a risk-neutral decision maker (DM).9 VOI analysis
\par
identifies the ``best'' information collection strategy as
\par
the one that yields the greatest net benefit to the DM.
\par
In their seminal book, Applied Statistical Decision
\par
Theory,
\par
5 Raiffa and Schlaifer introduced and mathe-
\par
matically defined the concept of the expected value of
\par
perfect information (EVPI), as shown in Equation 1:
\par
EVPI = E\textbraceleft maxa U(a,s)\textbraceright{} -- maxa E\textbraceleft U(a,s)\textbraceright, (1)
\par
where E\textbraceleft maxa U(a,s)\textbraceright{} represents the expected utility
\par
under perfect information (i.e., for each possible value
\par
of the uncertain input, s, the DM takes an action, a, that
\par
maximizes utility) and maxa E\textbraceleft U(a,s)\textbraceright{} represents the
\par
expected utility under prior information (i.e., the DM
\par
chooses the action that yields the highest expected util-
\par
ity without additional information). Calculating the ex-
\par
pected utility under perfect information requires as-
\par
sessing the optimal action for all possible values of s
\par
and then finding the weighted average of the resulting
\par
utility values over the prior belief about the likelihood
\par
of each event.
\par
As Howard6 stated, ``No theory that involves just the
\par
probabilities of outcomes without considering their
\par
consequences could possibly be adequate in describ-
\par
ing the importance of uncertainty to a decision maker.''
\par
Howard introduced and defined the concept of oppor-
\par
tunity loss, or regret, to explain EVPI. Under uncer-
\par
tainty, barring a dominant strategy, no matter what ac-
\par
tion the DM chooses, some chance exists that a
\par
different action might have yielded a higher utility on
\par
revelation of the true state of the world. In this framing,
\par
Howard defined the difference between utility given a
\par
priori action, a, and a posteriori optimal action, a*, as
\par
the opportunity loss of taking action under uncertainty
\par
(i.e., lacking perfect knowledge). Recognizing that the
\par
action that minimizes expected opportunity loss (EOL)
\par
also maximizes expected utility,
\par
mina EOL = mina [Es\textbraceleft U(a*,s) -- Es\textbraceleft U(a,s)\textbraceright ]
\par
= Es\textbraceleft U(a*,s)\textbraceright{} -- maxa Es\textbraceleft U(a,s)\textbraceright{} = EVPI. (2)
\par
\textbf{288 }{$\bullet$} MEDICAL DECISION MAKING/MAY--JUN 2004
\par
Thus, as long as the DM takes the ``best'' a priori action
\par
(i.e., the action that minimizes the expected opportu-
\par
nity loss given a priori information), the EOL equals the
\par
EVPI. Howard6 also introduced the concept of ``clair-
\par
voyance'' about a single uncertain input when multiple
\par
uncertainties exist in a model. The partial EVPI or the
\par
expected value of perfect X information (EVPXI)
\par
(where X represents a particular uncertain model in-
\par
put)10,11 represents the difference between the expected
\par
utility from taking the optimal action based on the rev-
\par
elation of the exact value of X and the expected utility
\par
from the optimal decision given only the prior informa-
\par
tion. Since EVPXI provides a useful measure for deter-
\par
mining the relative importance of resolving uncer-
\par
tainty of individual uncertain inputs (or more broadly,
\par
combinations of inputs), Felli and Hazen proposed its
\par
use as the ideal measure for sensitivity analysis in deci-
\par
sion analytic problems.12,13 However, the well-known
\par
nonadditivity of EVPXI estimates means that analysts
\par
cannot simply estimate an EVPXI (or all EVPXIs) from
\par
knowing the EVPI or vice versa (i.e., summing the
\par
EVPXI estimates fails to yield the total EVPI except in a
\par
very few limited cases).6,11,14
\par
Decision analysts also quickly recognized the im-
\par
possibility of obtaining perfect information in most
\par
cases, and consequently, they suggested that the ex-
\par
pected value of sample information (EVSI) or the ex-
\par
pected value of imperfect information (EVII) might rep-
\par
resent a more relevant measure. The EVSI represents
\par
the difference between the expected utility under im-
\par
perfect information (i.e., for each possible value of sam-
\par
ple information, x, the DM takes an action, a, that maxi-
\par
mizes utility) and the expected utility under prior
\par
information (i.e., the DM chooses the action that yields
\par
the highest expected utility without additional
\par
information):
\par
EVSI = EVII = Ex[maxa Es\textbar x\textbraceleft U(a,s)\textbraceright ] -- maxa Es\textbraceleft U(a,s)\textbraceright. (3)
\par
Calculating EVSI uses a Bayesian preposterior analysis
\par
since it requires making a decision before the collec-
\par
tion of information and knowledge of the sample out-
\par
come. Thus, it requires constructing posterior proba-
\par
bilities for all possible values of experimental results,
\par
finding the expected utility for taking the optimal ac-
\par
tion for each experimental result and taking a weighted
\par
average of the resulting utility values over the prior be-
\par
lief about the likelihood of each result. Analysts could
\par
similarly focus on estimating the expected value of
\par
sample X information (EVSXI).VALUE OF INFORMATION IN HEALTH RISK MANAGEMENT
\par
Since the EVPI represents both the simplest calcula-
\par
tion and the largest value (i.e., EVPI {$\geq$} max [EVPXI] and
\par
EVPI {$\geq$} EVSI {$\geq$} max [EVSXI]), it often serves as a useful
\par
upper bound for the value of any additional informa-
\par
tion in any particular decision. However, we note that
\par
even the EVPI may underestimate the true societal
\par
value of perfect information since positive externali-
\par
ties from information collection may exist (i.e., addi-
\par
tional decisions not directly modeled that may be im-
\par
proved from the information collected). For example,
\par
learning the truth about one patient's disease might
\par
lead to breakthroughs in epidemiological investiga-
\par
tions or in basic research (i.e., in addition to influenc-
\par
ing the decision about how to treat the patient). Fur-
\par
thermore, collecting additional information can lead to
\par
surprises in some cases where the information reveals
\par
incorrect basic assumptions (e.g., when observing in-
\par
put values outside the bounds of prior belief).15,16 None-
\par
theless, analysts often suggest that if the cost of collect-
\par
ing imperfect information exceeds the EVPI, then the
\par
DM should not invest in the proposed information
\par
collection strategy because collecting the information
\par
implies a net decrease in welfare.
\par
A prior review provides some important insights.
\par
Hilton17 found no general monotonic relationship be-
\par
tween VOI and action flexibility (i.e., increasing ac-
\par
tions available to the DM), level of DM's risk aversion,
\par
DM's wealth, or the level of initial uncertainty in the
\par
prior distribution, while emphasizing the ``model-
\par
specificity and meagerness of the general results in this
\par
area.'' A key insight from these properties emerges in
\par
that an analyst cannot ``conservatively'' model a VOI
\par
problem to bias VOI toward a low value; instead, ana-
\par
lysts must model each component to reflect the best
\par
available knowledge.
\par
Even to set up a very simple VOI problem, analysts
\par
must model the available set of actions, prior beliefs
\par
about the uncertain inputs and about the accuracy of
\par
the information collected, the consequences of actions
\par
given the true value of the uncertain inputs, and the
\par
DM's preferences. The prior belief about the uncertain
\par
inputs and the accuracy of information collected must
\par
be characterized using probability distributions (i.e.,
\par
discrete sets of value-probability pairs, parametric dis-
\par
tribution functions, empirical distribution func -
\par
tions).18 The analysis must quantify all relevant conse-
\par
quences of actions from the perspective of the DM and
\par
value monetary and nonmonetary outcomes using a
\par
common metric (typically net benefits in dollars in the
\par
context of VOI or perhaps net benefits in health units).
\par
For example, Weinstein and others19 outlined the ap-
\par
plication of the concept in which health represents the
\par
only outcome of concern, and Phelps and Mushlin20
\par
outlined a framework in medical technology assess-
\par
ment that accounts for the cost of the technology.
\par
With the problem set up, analysts use one of several
\par
strategies to obtain or estimate the solution to a VOI
\par
problem.21 These strategies include rolling back the de-
\par
cision tree for simple problems and using simulation
\par
and other numerical approximation methods for more
\par
complex problems. Clearly, the lack of a wealth of ap-
\par
plications in the literature suggests that as Howard7
\par
stated, ``Even an elementary problem . . . may be far
\par
from trivial in the familiarity with probabilistic opera-
\par
tions required to derive the results one would like to
\par
examine.'' Nonetheless, a critical mass of applications
\par
now exists, and this article represents a first effort to
\par
cut across disciplines and survey them in depth.
\par
SURVEY OF VOI APPLICATIONS
\par
We began the process of surveying the VOI applica-
\par
tions in the literature by identifying the studies that
\par
met our selection criteria. These criteria included 1)
\par
published in English in a peer-reviewed journal by the
\par
end of 2001, 2) included calculations by the authors of
\par
the key components of EVPI or EVSI, and 3) focused on
\par
a decision that involves management of a health risk.
\par
We conducted a literature search using 3 strategies:
\par
First, we searched for key words including value of in-
\par
formation, information value, value of perfect informa-
\par
tion, value of sample information, data worth, worth of
\par
data, and preposterior analysis in Medline and ISI Web
\par
of Science. Second, we searched the references cited in
\par
the identified VOI applications. Third, we searched the
\par
ISI Web of Science for all of the articles that cited a VOI
\par
application.
\par
We identified a total of 44 VOI applications in 42
\par
papers12,13,22--61 that met our entry criteria (Felli and
\par
Hazen12 include 3 separate applications in 1 article).
\par
Table 1 lists the 23 journals that published the applica-
\par
tions, with the corresponding number of papers found
\par
in each journal listed. The journals cover a wide range
\par
of topics, most with only 1 published VOI application
\par
and only 5 journals with 3 or more. We separated the
\par
applications into 2 fields (medical and environmental)
\par
to compare attributes across different disciplines. The
\par
medical field applications (18 papers) cover the fol-
\par
lowing topics:
\par
