puts. Remarkably, 25 of the applications (57\%)
\par
included only 1 uncertain input. Among the 5 topic ar-
\par
eas, the general environmental health and medical care
\par
applications showed the highest average number of
\par
uncertain inputs.
\par
Overall, about half of the applications we classified
\par
as general environmental health calculated only the
\par
expected value of perfect information (i.e., EVPI or
\par
EVPXI shown in the 5th and 7th columns of Table 2, re-
\par
spectively) and the other half only the expected value
\par
of sample information (i.e., EVSI or EVSXI shown in
\par
the 6th and 8th columns of Table 2, respectively). On
\par
the other hand, applications in toxicology focused
\par
mainly on expected value of sample information. Very
\par
few applications included calculations of the value of
\par
information from 1 of the sources of uncertainty (i.e.,
\par
EVPXI or EVSXI) (23\%), which appears very consistent
\par
with our finding that the majority of applications in-
\par
cluded only 1 source of uncertainty.
\par
We observed a wide range of solution strategies em-
\par
ployed by analysts, as shown in the 9th column of Ta-
\par
ble 2. Most notably, we see an apparent shift toward the
\par
use of simulation as the main solution strategy, with
\par
34\% using simulation, and all of these since 1990.
\par
Eleven applications (25\%) used a discrete distribution
\par
to characterize uncertainty and simple algebra or a dis-
\par
crete tree to solve the VOI problem; 6 applications
\par
(14\%) used a closed-form solution, 2 applications (5\%)
\par
used a method of moments approach, and 7 applica-
\par
tions (16\%) used various methods to discretize contin-
\par
uous distributions. For 3 applications (7\%), we could
\par
not determine the solution strategy based on the infor-
\par
mation in the published paper. We noted that applica-
\par
tions using simulation as a solution strategy generally
\par
showed higher numbers of uncertain inputs in their
\par
analyses and also appeared more likely to estimate
\par
EVPXI. We tested these using Spearman's rank correla-
\par
tion and found a statistically significant positive rank
\par
correlation between using simulation and the number
\par
of uncertain variables ({$\rho$} = 0.503, P = 0.0005) and be-
\par
tween using simulation and including calculation of
\par
EVPXI ({$\rho$} = 0.407, P = 0.0004).
\par
