We also tracked the citations and noted the lack of
\par
cross-fertilization between analysts who work in dif-
\par
ferent fields. Column 10 of Table 2 shows that 27 of the
\par
42 papers (64\%) cited a previously published VOI ap-
\par
plication, but a closer look reveals that this largely re-
\par
flects authors citing their own prior papers. Only 9 pa-
\par
pers (21\%) cite a VOI application that we identified as
\par
outside of the topic area of the application (column 11,
\par
Table 2). We also found that 21 (50\%) of the papers
\par
cited 1 of the original decision analysis texts by
\par
Howard or Raiffa and colleagues, as shown in the last
\par
column of Table 2.3--8
\par
Table 3 summarizes our overall findings, with the
\par
counts of the number of applications in each topic area
\par
characterized by the attributes listed in the leftmost
\par
column. As shown in the top section of Table 3, only
\par
57\% of the papers used the terminology information
\par
value, value of information, value of perfect informa-
\par
tion, or value of sample information in their title, ab-
\par
stract, or keywords, and only 77\% used any of these
\par
