\par
calculation in their analysis; however, James and oth-
\par
ers60 used an inferential approach based on the
\par
Kolmogorov-Smirnov statistic to rank the importance
\par
of uncertain inputs in contributing to opportunity loss.
\par
It appears that James and others60 lacked awareness of
\par
the EVPXI approach, and in the context of progressive
\par
refinement of the VOI methodology, it seems odd to re-
\par
vert to an inferential approach when analysts can di-
\par
rectly calculate the relative contribution of each uncer-
\par
tain input to decision error through EVPXI analysis.
\par
The difficulty in locating VOI applications under-
\par
scores the importance of gathering a comprehensive
\par
database to serve as a resource for decision analysis
\par
practitioners and DMs who want to explore the use of
\par
VOI techniques. VOI analysts need to ensure that fur-
\par
ther advancements in the methodology, such as the in-
\par
novative recent paper by Ades and Cliffe,64 benefit from
\par
the similar concepts discussed in other fields, for
\par
example, in this case, from the work of Brand and
\par
Small.10
\par
Our results suggest that the complexity of models, as
\par
measured by the number of uncertain inputs, correlates
\par
with the use of simulation as a solution strategy. How-
\par
ever, the complexity of models does not appear to be
\par
increasing over time (i.e., no statistically significant
\par
correlation appears with publication date). In a sepa-
\par
rate analysis, we focus on computational issues and the
\par
narrow environmental health risk set of applications.65
\par
The applications we reviewed primarily provide il-
\par
lustrations of VOI methods, and they do not typically
\par
represent true applications for real-world situations.
\par
This tendency may explain why many of the applica-
\par
tions include only 1 uncertain input, use only hypo-
\par
thetical data, apply generic values for consequences,
\par
and do not include a specific dollar year or a discount
\par
rate. Nonetheless, our finding that many applications
\par
failed to report critical information, including the per-
\par
spective taken, dollar year used, discount rate, time ho-
\par
rizon, and solution method, suggests the need for creat-
\par
ing some reporting standards. Without information
\par
about critical inputs and assumptions, replication of
\par
the results becomes impossible and analysts cannot
\par
learn from each other. We believe that all applications
\par
should minimally report a table of values used in the
\par
analysis (i.e., parameters of distribution functions and
\par
their sources) and discuss the strategy used to solve the
\par
METHODOLOGY \textbf{295}YOKOTA, THOMPSON
\par
VOI problem. With the current evolution of the meth-
\par
odology, we suggest convening an expert panel to dis-
\par
cuss the possibility of developing a reporting standard
\par
(similar to the effort that produced ref. 63). We hope
\par
that the list of attributes provided in this content
\par
analysis provides a useful starting point for discussion
\par
in such an effort.
\par
Other critical topics for discussion by such a panel
\par
include the issue of underestimating VOI. Although
\par
many applications characterize EVPI as an upper
\par
bound for EVSI, they ignore important limitations. For
\par
example, from the perspective of a DM who maximizes
\par
utility, the optimal information collection strategy rep-
\par
resents the one that yields the highest value, not the
\par
point at which the benefits outweigh the costs com-
\par
puted in threshold analyses. In addition, roughly 40\%
\par
of the applications do not take a societal perspective in
\par
their analyses, which may greatly underestimate the
\par
overall value of the information collected. For exam-
\par
ple, the clinical trial applications analyzed from the
\par
perspective of the provider do not account for the bene-
\par
fit of the information collected to other users of the
\par
drug or technology under study.33,36 None of the papers
\par
directly addressed the possibility that additional infor-
\par
mation would reveal overconfidence in the current
\par
characterization of uncertainty that would lead to an
\par
underestimation of the benefit of information
\par
collection in some cases.
\par
Valuation clearly represents another important area
\par
for discussion by analysts. Although cost-effectiveness
\par
analyses on medical diagnostic technologies abound,
\par
very few place a value per unit of effectiveness as re-
\par
quired to calculate VOI estimates, and as illustrated in
\par
Table 4, those that do might need to think more about
\par
the impact of time. As the popular nominal cutoff of
\par
\$50,000 illustrates, by using the same round-number
\par
cost-effectiveness threshold year after year, analysts
\par
systematically devalue the real value of a QALY. Simi-
\par
larly, environmental applications that focus on ``lives
\par
saved'' ignore morbidity, the length of life extension,
\par
and any ecological benefits. These applications sys-
\par
tematically undervalue reducing risks to children com-
\par
pared to the elderly and fail to capture the benefits of
\par
improving on nonfatal health effects or the
\par
environment.
\par
The assumption of risk neutrality also raises some
\par
interesting issues for further discussion. For example,
\par
although 3 applications in the general medical care
\par
area assumed no cost to the patient and considered
\par
only nonmonetized health benefits, in this case, risk
\par
neutrality remains undefined. Furthermore, those
\par
analyses that characterize only health impacts defining
\par
the value of information as the amount one would pay
\par
for information raises issues with how to transact any
\par
payment in health units.
\par
Additional applications for specific management
\par
decisions that address the important remaining chal-
\par
lenges promise to further advance VOI techniques and
\par
increase their use, and we hope that future studies find
\par
our analysis helpful.
\par
CONCLUSION
\par
As Howard6 noted,
\par
Placing a value on the reduction of uncertainty is the
\par
first step in experimental design, for only when we
\par
know what it is worth to reduce uncertainty do we have
\par
a basis for allocating our resources in experimentation
\par
designed to reduce the uncertainty.
\par
VOI analysis represents an important tool for any DM,
\par
program, or policy that considers collecting informa-
\par
tion to manage health risks. This article provides a
\par
comprehensive reference for analysts and DMs that
\par
may aid efforts to move past the demonstration phase
\par
of VOI into its real use in the context of actual health
\par
risk management decisions. In addition, this review
\par
provides an opportunity for researchers focused in one
\par
health risk area to take advantage of opportunities to
\par
learn from and build on the work of others. Although
\par
significant methodological issues remain in the con-
\par
text of solving very large and complicated problems fo-
\par
cused on assessing the value of actual sample data and
\par
dependent probabilities using dynamic Bayesian analy-
\par
ses in some cases, we remain optimistic about the value
\par
of VOI methodology. We expect that future syntheses
\par
will identify those papers that reported VOI applica-
\par
tions post-2001, including papers published prior to
\par
the actual publication of this study that we did not in-
\par
clude here given resource constraints.64,66
