\par
2.3. Agent-based social simulation
\par
ABSS is a computational technique that attempts to model human behaviour. Peo-
\par
ple, households or larger institutions are represented by a set of logical rules that deter-
\par
mine uniquely for each agent how they will act in a given environment (Edmonds et al.,
\par
1996; Chattoe, 2000). Although these rules, and the priorities of agents, are stated, the
\par
inclusion of non-linear variables, and the processes of interaction between agents, and
\par
between agents and their environment, allows novel behaviour to emerge.
\par
The agents can be said to hold opinions (beliefs about the environment or other
\par
agents based on limited knowledge), and these opinions may come to be held by an
\par
entire community through communication. Psychological, cultural and political con-
\par
straints can be included as determinants of how agents behave. The contextual pa-
\par
rameters can be specified and changed, which enables controlled experiments on
\par
the effect of changing social, economic and physical parameters to be measured
\par
(Moss, 1999). Non-economic motivations, such as preservation of environmental
\par
or cultural values, can enter into decisions, an important advantage over purely eco-
\par
nomically motivated models. This enables economic, social and biophysical elements
\par
to be considered in one model (Miller, 1998; Blench, 1999; Washington and Down-
\par
ing, 1999; Phillips et al., 2001). ABSS, therefore enables adaptation options to be
\par
evaluated; a growing concern for the climate change community (Smit et al.,
\par
2000). At the same time, the agents can have an internal metabolism and require-
\par
ments for food, shelter and other necessities. Rules both for farming activity and
\par
for economic behaviour govern the ability of individual agents to acquire these ne-
\par
cessities.
\par
ABSS facilitates anticipatory evaluation of technology adoption. It is an explor-
\par
atory method consistent with social science interests, as well as representing social
\par
behaviour in formal ways that link to models of natural systems. In exploratory
\par
models, dynamic behaviour is emphasised rather than equilibrium, which enables
\par
complex systems to be opened up so that rather than predicting outcomes, counter-
\par
intuitive behaviour can explored (Barreteau et al., 2001). ABSS has not been applied
\par
previously to seasonal forecasting, although other models have been used to
\par
address the anticipatory aspects of forecasts as emerging technology (Mazzocco
\par
et al., 1992).
\par
