3.3. Run 2: mean trust as a function of forecast accuracy
\par
So far we have examined the consequences for a farmer-agent who blindly follows
\par
a forecast when it is issued, irrespective of the impact on their productivity. The role-
\par
play exercise in Ha Tlhaku suggests that farmers become more likely to accept a fore-
\par
cast as familiarity increases, and that forecasts of poor conditions tend to be ignored
\par
(see Section 2.2). On the other hand, farmers are likely to lose confidence in the fore-
\par
cast if it fails often, or if they suffer from poor returns as a result of following a fore-
\par
cast of good conditions that turns out to be incorrect. As a model for this we allocate a
\par
floating point number to each farmer-agent that represents their level of trust in the
\par
forecast. The trust increases linearly by one unit per year whenever the forecast proves
\par
correct and decreases by the same amount when it is not. After 3 years, the farmer-
\par
