3.4. Run 3: collective effects
\par
We now ask how things might change when there is a range of behaviour and
\par
wealth in a population, and how this might be changed by social interactions within
\par
such a setting. We restrict ourselves, in this section, to the case where the forecast is
\par
correct 60\% of the time and incorrect by two terciles (i.e., as bad as possible) at most
\par
10\% of the time.
\par
We create a ``village'' of 700 household-agents, which are selected from one of the
\par
three household classes, each with a different initial store of grain, number of fields
\par
and household members. The household classes (better-off, average, poor) reflect the
\par
typical distribution of wealth in Lesotho and are based on field work and literature
\par
on Lesotho (Gay and Hall, 2000; Turner et al., 2001 -- see Table 7). Separate to this,
\par
each household has an intrinsic sensitivity to the forecast, and a tendency to pay at-
\par
tention to the opinions of others.
\par
For initial storage, we assume a Gaussian distribution, with variance of 0.2 ton-
\par
nes, and mean as given in the table. We distribute the rate of increase of trust, again
\par
using a Gaussian distribution, with mean 0.5 and variance 0.2. Thus, only those on
\par
the more optimistic fringe of the distribution increase their trust at the rate of pre-
\par
vious sections. We use the same distribution for the drop in trust that occurs when
\par
a forecast fails, although individual farmer-agents may have different values for their
\par
rates of increase and decrease of trust. The `once bitten, twice shy' behaviour is also
\par
given a Gaussian spread -- when the forecast for a wet year proves dry, farmer-agents
\par
drop their trust to a mean level of 2.5 and variance again of 0.2.
\par
