4. Discussion and suggestions for further work
\par
4.1. Build up of trust
\par
A somewhat surprising result in the above is the long time scale over which trust
\par
builds up, despite the three-year rate at which farmer-agents learn to believe in the
\par
forecast. The forecast accuracy is important, as can be seen from the following sim-
\par
ple argument:
\par
Suppose that trust T increases linearly at rate i if the forecast is correct, and de-
\par
creases linearly at rate d if the forecast is incorrect, and that the forecast is correct a
\par
fraction p of the time. Then we can write for the mean trust hT i (over many se-
\par
quences) after N years:G. Ziervogel et al. = Agricultural Systems 83 (2005) 1--26 21
