\par
4.2. Suggestions for further work
\par
There are a number of areas where the model described here could be extended.
\par
First, there is a simplified representation of climate -- rainfall is divided into three cat-
\par
egories sampled with a crude frequency distribution, although such information is
\par
typical of the level of detail available from consensus forecasts. Further, we take
\par
no account of the effect of extremes that might lead to across the board crop failure
\par
for all household classes over an entire country.
\par
Second, the crop parametrization is highly simplified. To begin with, we have al-
\par
lowed only for variation in rainfall, when temperature may have serious effects (un-
\par
expected frosts may destroy an entire crop). A more complete representation of crop
\par
response to climate is clearly called for (see, e.g., Challinor et al., 2003, for an exam-
\par
ple in the case of ground nut in India).
\par
Third, although the response of people to the forecast itself and to each other has
\par
been addressed in the model, it could be explored further. In the role-play a number
\par
of response options were given and two of the most common ones were chosen for
\par
use in the model. Given a crop model, we can say what the best response in a given
\par
climate would be, but is this in fact what would be adopted on the ground? Farmers
\par
may be reluctant to risk changes in farming practice in the face of untried new infor-
\par
mation. Or, they may experiment with only part of their fields, reducing the costs of
\par
poor forecasts. Moreover, the perception of the forecast must be set in the context of
\par
local culture. Traditional beliefs about the climate and its representation in the local22 G. Ziervogel et al. = Agricultural Systems 83 (2005) 1--26
\par
environment may conflict with those suggested by the forecast. Although this was
\par
only a problem for a small proportion of the role-play participants, cultural objec-
\par
tion may be stronger in other regions. The presentation of the forecast by extension
\par
officers, and the role of the village chief may either accelerate or hinder forecast
\par
adoption. Further field work is needed to try to find ways in which these kinds of
\par
effects can be faithfully rendered by the modelling process. In the context of interac-
\par
tion, more is needed in the exploration of opinion formation. It may be that al-
\par
though only a few people are made worse off by a good forecast, they are vocal
\par
or important community members. Such people may have a disproportionate effect
\par
on forecast impact.
\par
Fourth, the economy, either local or global, is hardly represented in the current
\par
case. If people run out of food, are they able to buy it in? Is there sufficient local sup-
\par
ply to allow this to happen? Many families own animals and gain income in other
\par
ways than through farming, either by share-cropping, selling whatever they can
\par
make from local materials, or else by seeking work outside the village. All of these
\par
factors not only determine how vulnerable a household may be to crop failure,
\par
but will affect their attitude to and ability to take on risk, and thus their ability to
\par
deal with the possible benefits of the forecast.
\par
Last, finer time steps of farmer-agent responses could be included. For example,
\par
monthly timesteps could be incorporated to enable the initial strategy to be updated
\par
depending on how the predicted and actual climate compare through the season.
\par
This could enable disseminators to assess what impacts an improvement on the tim-
\par
ing of dissemination would have, something users are concerned with.
\par
5. Conclusions
\par
The paper set out to achieve three aims. The first aim was to evaluate the pay-offs
\par
of using the forecast as a function of forecast accuracy. The model shows that unless
\par
the forecast accuracy is 60--70\% or above, positive impacts from using forecasts are
\par
unlikely. Forecast accuracy is therefore paramount in determining the direct benefit
\par
of forecast use to marginal farmers. Additional to this, the model suggests that non-
\par
linearities in crop response (in this case to rainfall) are crucial to the usability of fore-
\par
casts.
\par
The second aim was to establish how the mean trust of individual farmer-agents
\par
changes as a function of forecast accuracy. The results show that the level of forecast
\par
accuracy determines the level of trust and that there is a threshold of below which
\par
trust will be lost significantly. If the forecast is accurate 60\% of the time and very
\par
poor not more than 10\% of the time, then trust saturates at 30 years. For some
\par
household-agents, the mean trust level saturates at the point where using the forecast
\par
begins to become economically advantageous. Many household-agents, if acting in
\par
isolation, may give up using forecasts earlier because of short-term losses, even
\par
though in the longer term using forecasts may be to their benefit.
\par
The third aim was to measure the benefit of using the forecast in terms of yield,
\par
with and without farmer-farmer interaction and for different wealth profiles. ResultsG. Ziervogel et al. = Agricultural Systems 83 (2005) 1--26 23
\par
indicate that social processes have an impact on the effect of the forecast, both from
\par
the point of view of individual response and that of interaction between individuals.
\par
Poor farmer-agents benefit the most in the sense of reducing the likelihood of food
\par
shortage conditions (cf. Figs. 5 and 8(a) and (b)), but yield enhancement is better for
\par
the wealthier household classes.
\par
The ABSS model highlights some of the potential impacts that seasonal forecasts
\par
might have on rural households in Lesotho. Since the model explores possible out-
\par
comes of individuals using information that they do not presently use, the results
\par
are hard to validate. However, field work and theoretical evidence supports many
\par
of the results. It suggests that seasonal climate forecasts are not a panacea to improv-
\par
ing poor households' rural livelihoods.
\par
Although the model is a pilot and based on data from one area in Lesotho, it pro-
\par
vides a holistic approach to assessing the impact of anticipatory information. The
\par
model is not being used for policy at this stage, so its merit is more in the exploratory
\par
realm of investigating possible ramifications of using a forecast of varying skill in dif-
\par
ferent ways. Policies for sustainability cannot wait until all facts are known. Rather,
\par
we need to use information that is as robust as possible to plan and implement
\par
changes in technology and activities. By explicitly documenting the steps and asso-
\par
ciated assumptions used to produce the model, as well as listing the simplifications,
\par
we hope that it can be re-evaluated for another case, or if different factors are a pri-
\par
ority that these can be changed at the appropriate level.
\par
The evolution of trust highlights the importance of understanding the human
\par
agency of users and how it varies. Opinion formation and the establishment of trust
\par
is complex and forecast disseminators cannot expect to have credibility if they do not
\par
explain the probabilistic nature of the forecast and past forecast skill. The results
\par
also imply that a certain level of forecast skill is required in order for trust not to
\par
decline steadily. Forecast centres and disseminators might consider improving fore-
\par
cast skill before disseminating seasonal forecasts. They may also need to recognise
\par
that large forecast errors have differential impacts on trust. If the climate prediction
\par
is bi-modal with low confidence, forecast centres may wish to make sure users are
\par
aware of the uncertainties. If trust is lost, it may take some time be regained.
\par
The model draws attention to the fact that villages cannot be treated as homog-
\par
enous. The impact of the forecast on better-off and poor households differs signifi-
\par
cantly. If national level forecast dissemination strategies are prioritised, the model
\par
suggests that blanket targeting of rural groups will not necessarily address user
\par
needs. Further investigation into the impact of the forecast at the village level is
\par
encouraged before dissemination is actively pursued without appreciating potential
\par
impacts.
